Code list snapshots (rules/en16931/1.3.16/codelists/)
  The membership tests of the EN 16931 rule pack are decided against dated
  snapshots of published code lists. Each snapshot file carries a list of codes and
  the short names their publisher gives them, and no prose of any publisher is
  reproduced. They were extracted mechanically on 2026-09-19 from four
  publications, none of which states a licence for the data on the page it is
  offered from; rules/en16931/1.3.16/codelists/SOURCES.md records the URL, the
  SHA-256 of the bytes downloaded and what was found on the page, and records the
  uncertainty about the terms as an open question rather than an answer.

    - The listing of the code lists used in EN 16931, the Electronic Address Scheme
      code list and the VAT Exemption Reason code list, published by the European
      Commission in its registry of supporting artefacts for EN 16931. These are the
      source of the UNTDID, ISO/IEC 6523 ICD, country and unit of measure snapshots,
      which are the Commission's statement of which codes of each list this standard
      admits; the underlying lists are published by UN/CEFACT, UNECE and ISO.
    - The ISO 4217 currency code table, published by SIX Group as the ISO 4217
      maintenance agency.
    - The ISO 639-1 language code vocabulary, published by the Library of Congress
      as the ISO 639-2 registration authority.
    - The media types registry, published by IANA. Only the six media types that
      EN 16931-1 admits for an attachment are carried, each looked up in that
      registry.

  The same codes and publisher names travel a second time in the generated enums of
  esj-invoice (de.bsnsoft.esj.invoice.code), which are emitted from these
  snapshot files and carry nothing that is not in them.

  No code list in this repository is taken from the code list files distributed with
  the CEN validation artefacts, which are EUPL-1.2 material.

Country names (esj-render/src/main/resources/de/bsnsoft/esj/render/
names/country.json)
  The letter layout of esj-render writes the name of a country where an address
  carries a country code. Those names are the ones of the Unicode Common Locale
  Data Repository (CLDR, https://cldr.unicode.org/), read once out of the locale
  data of a Java runtime by the generator CountryNames of esj-render and checked
  in as data. They are used under the Unicode Terms of Use / Unicode License v3,
  https://www.unicode.org/license.txt, which permits use and redistribution of the
  Unicode Data Files with the copyright notice and the permission notice:

    Copyright (c) 1991-Present Unicode, Inc. All rights reserved.
    Distributed under the Terms of Use in https://www.unicode.org/copyright.html.

  Only names are carried; no other part of the CLDR and no prose of it is
  reproduced. The header of the file records the runtime the names were read from,
  and a test regenerates and compares on that runtime. The renderer never reads
  the locale data of the machine it runs on, so a rendering is the same bytes
  everywhere.

  The four other tables beside that file — invoice type, unit of measure, means of
  payment and VAT category — carry the English names of the dated code list
  snapshots of rules/en16931/1.3.16/codelists/ named above, under the entry for
  those snapshots, and German names written for this project.

